;; Genesis: book-level decision notes. Format: gsb/translation/README.md

(note "**Text.** Swete's Septuagint (our transcription), which essentially follows Codex Vaticanus. Where these notes say a reading \"differs from the Hebrew\", the comparison is with the standard (Masoretic) Hebrew text behind most English Bibles, made from the translator's own knowledge for the owner to check; no copyrighted translation is quoted or used. Notes so marked begin **Differs from the Hebrew.**; notes on New Testament quotations and echoes begin **Quoted in the NT.** and compare the Greek of both from our own tables.")

(note "**Lemmas we supplied.** About one word in twelve in Genesis 1-3 has no lemma in the table, because the form does not occur in the New Testament data. Those lemmas were supplied by hand with the (N sense \"lemma\") form and are listed in the concordance whys as \"(supplied)\". Where the table's guessed lemma (flagged lemma?) was wrong, it is overridden and noted on the word (2:11 οὗ \"where\"; 2:25 and 3:8 ὅ τε, the article, not the relative).")

(note "**ἄνθρωπος and Adam.** We translate the Greek word in each place. ἄνθρωπος (a human being) is \"human\" in the literal layer and \"man\" in the readable layer, never \"Adam\". Ἀδάμ is \"Adam\". The Septuagint's own switch from ἄνθρωπος to Ἀδάμ comes at 2:16; the Hebrew text says \"the man\" in most of those places, so the name appears earlier in our English than in translations from the Hebrew. The Greek's article with the name (τὸν Ἀδάμ) is aligned and unrendered.")

(note "**The divine name.** θεός is \"God\" (the Greek article with it is unrendered); Κύριος ὁ θεός is \"the Lord God\", one segment, with the article before θεός carrying the English \"the\" at the front. We do not print \"LORD\" in small capitals: Κύριος is the same word whether it stands for the divine name or not, and the Greek has no typographic distinction.")

(note "**Pronouns from verb endings.** A Greek finite verb includes its subject's person and number. When English needs a subject pronoun for it (\"he rested\", \"let it be\", \"it came to be so\"), the pronoun is part of the verb's segment and is not bracketed, as a case ending's English preposition is part of its noun's segment (John book notes). Its English gender (he/she/it) is a translator's choice the Greek ending does not make, so where the referent is open the note says so. A pronoun the Greek writes as a separate word (αὐτός, οὗτος) is aligned to that word.")

(note "**Tense, aspect and mood.** As in John: present indicative progressive (\"is going out\"), imperfect \"was ...ing\" (\"was being borne\", \"was watering\"), aorist simple past, perfect \"has ...\" (\"has come to be\", \"I have given\"). Added for Genesis: aorist participles \"having ...ed\" (\"having taken\"), present participles \"...ing\"; infinitives \"to ...\", and an article before an infinitive is unrendered (πρὸ τοῦ γενέσθαι, \"before [it] came to be\"). Imperatives, present or aorist, are plain English imperatives (\"Grow and multiply\", \"fill\"); third-person imperatives are \"let ...\". English cannot show aspect in a command without distortion; whether the literal layer should try (\"keep growing\") is open for the owner. The future used for permission or command (\"you will eat\") stays future in the literal layer.")

(note "**Particles.** καί is always \"and\", including at the start of a sentence; δέ is always \"but\"; γάρ \"for\"; τε \"and\" or, with καί, \"both ... and\"; ἰδού \"behold\". Postpositive particles move to the front of their English clause. μέν does not occur in Genesis 1-3. The readable layer may soften δέ to \"now\" or \"and\".")

(note "**The article.** Rendered \"the\" wherever English can carry it, including with abstract nouns (\"the tree of the life\"). Unrendered (aligned, not translated) in four cases, each a named sense in the concordance: before God or a personal name; the second article of the attributive pattern (\"the great luminaries\"); before an infinitive; and where a possessive or demonstrative takes its place (\"his works\", \"that land\"). Articles the Greek lacks are bracketed: \"[the]\", \"[a]\".")

(note "**Hebraisms kept in the literal layer.** The Septuagint often reproduces Hebrew idiom word for word, and the literal layer keeps it so the reader sees the Greek as it is: \"came to be into a living soul\" (2:7), \"the two will be into one flesh\" (2:24), \"with eating you will eat\" (2:16), \"by death you will die\" (2:17), \"multiplying I will multiply\" (3:16), \"from [the] face of\" (3:8), \"of which the seed of it [is] in it\" (1:11). The readable layer turns them into English (\"became\", \"freely eat\", \"surely die\", \"greatly multiply\").")

(note "**Split and partly supplied segments.** The Genesis 1-3 pilot found English shapes the first format could not write; the format now allows them (gsb/translation/README.md). One Greek word rendered by English pieces that sit apart is written as one segment plus a continuation, (cont N): \"Let [a] firmament come to be\" (1:6), \"sent him out\" (3:23). A segment may hold a supplied word beside translated ones: \"of [the] field\" (2:5), \"leaves of [a] fig tree\" (3:7). A supplied word may carry punctuation: \"[one].\" (2:23).")
